# Project overview

Flask monolith finance tracker (loans + spending + daily budget). No separate frontend framework.

## Layout

- `@app.py` — routes, domain logic, JSON persistence (large single module)
- `templates/` — Jinja pages (`daily_budget.html`, `spending.html`, `spending_search.html`, `loans.html`, …)
- `static/` — vanilla JS/CSS (`daily_budget.js`, `spending.js`, `spending_search.js`, `script.js`, `style.css`)
- `tests/` — `unittest` modules (not pytest-first; run with `python -m unittest …`)
- `data.json` — local persistence (gitignored); production has its own copy + `.env`
- `.cursor/rules/` — agent context; keep learnings here for future agents

## Stack / runtime

- Python Flask app: `python app.py` or `./start.sh` (sources `.env` if present)
- Auth users from `FINANCE_TRACKER_USERS` (`user:pass,user2:pass2`); if unset, local defaults `admin`/`admin123` and `user`/`user123`
- Session login required for app routes; APIs return JSON
- Static assets may be cache-busted via helpers in `app.py` — hard-refresh if UI looks stale after deploy

## Data model (high level)

- Top-level JSON: `{ "users": { "<username>": { "spending": … } }, "loans": … }`
- Per-user spending lives under `users.<user>.spending` (`transactions`, `statements`, `monthly_insights`, `daily_budget`)
- Manual daily spends are normal outgoing transactions with `source: "manual"` and a `date` (`YYYY-MM-DD`)

## Statement import dates

- **Upload surface is Reconcile** (`/spending/reconcile`) — Home has no import form. Home Statements lists months; every row opens Reconcile.
- Extracts optional `started_date` / `completed_date` (e.g. Revolut) alongside `date`
- Ledger `date` prefers **completed/settled** when present (`_resolve_spending_transaction_dates`)
- Period filter keeps rows by that ledger date; rows whose `started_date` is outside the range are tagged `date_boundary`. Reconcile composer: collapsed **Adjust date range** (`#reconcile-period-start` / `#reconcile-period-end`), sent with upload.
- Supported uploads: PDF, CSV, TXT, Excel **`.xlsx`** (converted via `openpyxl` → CSV-like text). File inputs must include `.xlsx` + `application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet` in `accept` or mobile pickers hide Excel files. Old `.xls` is rejected with a clear error.
- **Structured CSV/XLSX** are parsed **locally** after mapping headers: optional alias fast path, else a **tiny LLM call on header titles only** (`_llm_map_tabular_headers`) that returns column indices + amount-sign convention — never sends row data. Amount-sign from headers alone is provisional: after columns are mapped, `_infer_amount_sign_from_data_rows` samples the Amount column and treats the **majority sign as outgoing** (so Amex-style positive charges stay outgoing; Revolut-style negative spend stays outgoing). PENDING/FAILED states are skipped when a state column is mapped.
- Unstructured PDFs still use layout hints and/or chunked LLM extraction (`STATEMENT_LLM_CHUNK_CHARS`, default extraction timeout **90s** per chunk via `OPENAI_EXTRACTION_TIMEOUT`).
- Helpers: `_extract_spreadsheet_text`, `_is_spreadsheet_filename`, `_try_parse_tabular_spending_transactions`, `_llm_map_tabular_headers`, `_infer_amount_sign_from_data_rows`, `_split_statement_text_into_llm_chunks`; tests: `@tests/test_statement_xlsx.py`, `@tests/test_statement_date_boundary.py`

## Tests

```sh
# Full suite (required before handing off a feature PR)
python -m unittest discover -s tests -v

# Focused examples
.venv/bin/python -m unittest tests.test_daily_budget tests.test_daily_budget_ui -v
```

Prefer extending existing unittest files; UI presence checks often assert substrings in rendered HTML + JS source.

## Feature handoff checklist (standard for every new feature)

Before considering a feature done:

1. **Run all unit tests** — `python -m unittest discover -s tests -v` must pass.
2. **Step-by-step UI playthrough** — if the change is user-visible, exercise the real flow end-to-end and capture screenshots as PR evidence (see below). Non-UI / backend-only changes still need the full test run; playthrough screenshots only when UX changed.
3. **Commit + push** screenshot assets under `docs/screenshots/<feature>/` on the PR branch before updating the PR description.
4. **Update agent rules** when you learn durable repo facts (APIs, invariants, UX contracts).

## PR demo screenshots (step-by-step playthrough)

For PRs that change user-visible UI, **do not** ship only a single static screenshot of the finished state. Carry out a **step-by-step playthrough** of the feature as a user would, and add those screenshots **inline in the PR body** as ordered evidence (visible without opening another page).

Cover the meaningful steps of the flow, for example:

- Starting state / empty or prior data
- Reusing an existing value or control (when the feature has suggestions, history, or defaults)
- Creating or adding something **new** (new label, new source, new entry, etc.)
- Confirm / save / import (mid-flow “ready to submit” when useful)
- After-save result (list, table, success state, updated suggestions)
- Any filter/sort/follow-on view that proves the new data stuck

How to capture and embed:

1. Drive the playthrough with a Playwright helper under `tmp/screenshot_*.py` (see existing `tmp/screenshot_*.py` scripts; bank-source flow is a good full-playthrough example).
2. Commit copies under `docs/screenshots/<feature>/` and push the branch.
3. In the PR body, present them as a numbered **Demo — full flow** (or similar) with HTML `<img>` tags using **`raw.githubusercontent.com`** URLs for this branch, e.g.  
   `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Ivaneres/loan-tracker/<branch>/docs/screenshots/<feature>/<file>.png`
4. Do **not** use Cursor artifact `/tmp/cursor/artifacts/…` paths or markdown links to `cursor.com/agents/…/artifacts` — those become click-through links, not inline images.
5. Prefer tight crops of the changed control for each step, plus one full mobile/desktop frame when helpful.

